<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Siege</p><h1 class="enc-title">Siege of Turin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1706">1706</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Siege of Turin (1706) · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a> was the French attempt in 1706 to capture the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-amadeus-ii">Victor Amadeus II</a> of Savoy during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a>. After a siege of nearly four months, a relief army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-eugene-of-savoy">Prince Eugene of Savoy</a> joined the duke and defeated the French besiegers on 7 September 1706. The victory drove the French out of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and is commemorated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basilica-of-superga">Basilica of Superga</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/siege-of-turin.jpg" alt="Siege of Turin" width="320" height="164" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Karl von Blaas · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karl_von_Blaas_-_Die_Schlacht_bei_Turin_1706_-_2734_-_Kunsthistorisches_Museum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
